  "explanation": "LSR = d(depth)/d(age). The seven rows above p99 (row indices 411-417, 240.41-241.88 ka, 4.20-4.27 m, 6 with d18o) form ONE contiguous episode; no other episode exists, so it is unique under the per-row p99 rule. It is NOT caused by an enlarged depth step: six of the seven rows sit on ordinary 0.01 m steps, and although the 411->412 interval carries a 0.02 m step (one of five such rows in the record: 44, 63, 312, 370, 411), none of those five intervals is itself extreme (their LSRs are 0.0128-0.0480 m/ka, and three are below the median). The spike is instead an age-grid artefact: age increments inside 240.87-241.88 ka are 0.20-0.23 ka against a record median of 0.45 ka, so the same 0.01 m sampling yields ~2.3x the median LSR. Counterfactual: replacing only the compressed age increments at intervals [412, 413, 414, 415, 416] by the median step, with the depth grid untouched, collapses the record max from 0.05000 to 0.04348 m/ka and leaves only 3 rows above the new p99. Competing explanation confirmed: the extreme LSR is a sampling/age-model artefact (unusually fine age increments on an ordinary 0.01 m depth grid), not an enlarged depth step and not established as a real sedimentation pulse; the spike is not generated by the depth grid. Under the alternative 10 ka block-average definition the 240-250 ka bin is flagged (rank 2 of 36 bins, mean LSR 0.0338 m/ka vs bin p95 0.0333) but the 80-90 ka bin is higher (0.0348 m/ka), so the 241 ka episode is no longer the single standout (it is the 2nd-highest of 2 flagged bins, both marginally above the 95th percentile); using the more robust within-bin depth-span/age-span rate instead of the row mean the 240-250 ka bin is not flagged at all (rank 6 of 36), confirming that at the 10 ka scale the episode is not exceptional. Its d18o anomaly is -0.170 per mille (-0.49 sd of the surrounding 20 ka window, i.e. well within the window spread), and refitting the dominant sinusoid with the episode removed changes the best period by 0.1 ka and R^2 by +0.034, so this short interval does not control the record-wide fit.",
